\section{Training Objective Design}

\paragraph{What the controller observes.}
Both controllers use the temporal context design of MetaMix~\cite{wang2023metamix}. Before the classifier update, we compute mean cross-entropy on the clean current batch and the clean labeled replay batch. Let $g_t$ and $g_r$ be their gradients with respect to the classifier parameters, and let $L_r$ be the replay cross-entropy. Each LSTM input concatenates these batch statistics with individual pooled features:
\begin{equation}
 I_i=[g_t^\top g_r,\;L_r,\;e_{r,i},\;e_{t,i}]
 \in\mathbb{R}^{258}.
\end{equation}
Here $e_{t,i}$ and $e_{r,i}$ are 128-dimensional features from clean current and replay images. The gradient dot product measures whether their batch losses favor locally compatible updates: a positive value indicates that a sufficiently small step along the negative current gradient would decrease replay loss to first order, whereas a negative value indicates conflict. Replay loss summarizes how well the classifier currently fits remembered examples, and the feature vectors provide image-specific context.

The two scalars are shared across all inputs in the batch. Each current example is paired with a replay feature by batch position, and each replay example with a current feature; pairing cycles through the shorter batch when their sizes differ. This is positional pairing, not a same-class match. When memory is empty, replay features, replay loss, and the gradient dot product are zero. The context is detached from the classifier and passed to an LSTM with five hidden units. Hidden and cell states persist in separate current and replay batch-position slots and are detached between batches; they summarize earlier steps without tracking individual image identities.

The MetaMix baseline maps the LSTM hidden state to three chain weights shared across views and operation pools for each example. One variant also learns $m$, while the other uses the prescribed sweep value. Because the LSTM context contains no sampled-chain information, resampling chains at fixed context and recurrent state leaves these weights unchanged.

The chain-aware controller uses the same LSTM context and the prescribed $m$ for both operation pools. For each seen chain, it concatenates the change in pooled features with the change in class probabilities relative to the clean input, giving a detached 138-dimensional descriptor. This descriptor enters a separate shared scorer together with the five-dimensional LSTM hidden state; it is not an additional LSTM input. A softmax over the three scores supplies weights separately for each example and seen view. For each pseudo-unseen view we draw $w\sim\mathrm{Dirichlet}(1,1,1)$ independently of the controller and keep the draw fixed throughout the batch's mixer updates.

\paragraph{What the classifier learns.}
Write $\ell_\theta(x,y)=\CE(f_\theta(x),y)$ for mean batch cross-entropy, where $f_\theta$ returns logits. A separate replay draw $(x_d,z_d)$ supplies stored logits for distillation. Our DER++ loss~\cite{buzzega2020dark} is
\begin{align}
 L_{\rm DER++}(\theta)
 &=\ell_\theta(x_t,y_t)+0.5\,\ell_\theta(x_r,y_r)\notag\\
 &\quad+0.2\,\mathrm{MSE}(f_\theta(x_d),z_d).
\end{align}
MSE averages over examples and logit coordinates. Replay terms are zero when memory is empty. The classifier objective is
\begin{equation}
 L_S(\theta,\phi)=L_{\rm DER++}(\theta)
       + C_\theta(x_b,x'_{b1},x'_{b2}).
 \label{eq:support}
\end{equation}
Here $C_\theta$ is either JS or class-aware InfoNCE; the latter also uses the labels $y_b$. The dependence on $\phi$ enters through the mixed views.

For three aligned batches $v_0,v_1,v_2$ of size $n$, let $p_{ij}$ denote the classifier's probability vector for example $i$ in view $v_j$, and let $\bar p_i=(p_{i0}+p_{i1}+p_{i2})/3$. We use
\begin{equation}
 \mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{JS},\theta}(v_0,v_1,v_2)
 =\frac{1}{3n}\sum_{i=1}^n\sum_{j=0}^2\KL(p_{ij}\,\|\,\bar p_i).
 \label{eq:three-js}
\end{equation}
For InfoNCE, index the $3n$ views by $a$ and let $u_a$ be the normalized pooled feature. The positive set $P_a$ contains the other two views of the same batch entry; $N_a$ contains all views with a different class label. At temperature $\tau=0.1$,
\begin{equation}
 \mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{NCE},\theta}=-\frac{1}{3n}\sum_a\frac{1}{2}\sum_{b\in P_a}
 \log\frac{\exp(u_a^\top u_b/\tau)}
 {\sum_{c\in P_a\cup N_a}\exp(u_a^\top u_c/\tau)}.
 \label{eq:seen-nce}
\end{equation}

\paragraph{How the outer loss reaches the mixer.}
After one virtual classifier step of size $\alpha$, we evaluate clean classification and pseudo-unseen consistency on the same batch:
\begin{align}
 \theta^+(\phi)&=\theta-\alpha\nabla_\theta L_S(\theta,\phi),\label{eq:virtual-step}\\
 L_U(\theta^+,\phi)&=\ell_{\theta^+}(x_b,y_b)
    +\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{JS},\theta^+}(x_b,\hat x_{b1},\hat x_{b2}).\label{eq:outer}
\end{align}
The mixer minimizes $J(\phi)=L_U(\theta^+(\phi),\phi)$.
Holding the sampled batch, incoming state and outer BatchNorm buffers fixed, the chain rule gives
\begin{equation}
 \nabla_\phi J=(D_\phi\theta^+)^\top\nabla_{\theta^+}L_U
                +\partial_\phi L_U .
\end{equation}
The partial derivative holds the virtual classifier fixed. For chain-aware mixing the outer images are independent of $\phi$, so this term vanishes. For MetaMix the pseudo-unseen images depend on learned chain weights even when $m$ is fixed, so a direct JS gradient can remain. Clean cross-entropy reaches either mixer through the classifier update. At $m=1$ the three inputs coincide and their JS is zero under deterministic evaluation. This identity describes the consistency term alone.

Three mixer updates recompute the virtual classifier from the same starting parameters. The classifier commits the step computed with the incoming mixer once per batch. This is the full derivative of one SGD step. The MetaMix baseline uses the project's interpretation of the published solver.

\section{Experimental Protocol}
The 288,746-parameter SmallCNN learns five successive CIFAR-10 class pairs, $(0,1)$ through $(8,9)$, with ten epochs per task and a 500-example reservoir. Each batch has up to 32 current examples and up to 32 labeled replay examples. The classifier and mixer use constant SGD learning rates of 0.003 and 0.005. Each run processes 500,000 current-image presentations in 15,650 classifier updates.

Final evaluation uses 10,000 clean test images and 15 CIFAR-10-C corruptions at five severities~\cite{hendrycks2019benchmarking}. Each corruption and severity receives equal weight. Class-IL predicts among all ten outputs. Task-IL restricts prediction to the true class pair. The pseudo-unseen operations used in training are sampled training transformations. CIFAR-10-C supplies only the test measurements.

\paragraph{References and aggregation.}
DER++ with AugMix samples two views from the full nine-operation pool, with independent $m\sim\mathrm{Beta}(1,1)$ and chain weights drawn from $\mathrm{Dirichlet}(1,1,1)$. We evaluate its original JS coefficient of 12 and a coefficient of one matched to the JS classifiers. Both use the same replay and classifier update budgets. Each mean gives equal weight to seeds 17, 23, and 43. We report sample SD across seeds in Table~\ref{tab:sd} and compute differences from the displayed means.

\section{Results}
\paragraph{Mixture strength across four metrics.}
For chain-aware InfoNCE all four means decline as $m$ increases (Table~\ref{tab:main}). The largest class-IL difference occurs on corruptions: 43.89\% at $m=0.1$ versus 34.18\% at $m=0.9$. MetaMix InfoNCE reaches its highest class-IL means at $m=0.3$ and its highest task-IL means at $m=0.1$. With JS, the highest corrupted class-IL mean occurs at $m=0.1$ for both mixers, although intermediate values give a less regular ordering.

\paragraph{What the chain-aware design adds.}
With classifier InfoNCE and $m=0.1$, chain-aware leads on all four metrics. At $m=0.3$ MetaMix leads on corrupted class-IL by 0.23 points, while chain-aware leads on the other three metrics. At $m=0.5$ their clean task-IL means coincide to two decimals. The comparison changes both seen-chain scoring and pseudo-unseen mixture construction.

\paragraph{Interaction with classifier consistency.}
Table~\ref{tab:delta} holds the mixer and mixture strength fixed when comparing classifier losses. Chain-aware gains 5.69 and 5.95 points on corrupted class-IL from InfoNCE at $m=0.1$ and $0.3$, compared with 0.93 at $m=0.5$. MetaMix gains 8.20 points at $m=0.3$ but loses 1.74 at $m=0.9$. The gains also depend on task information: at $m=0.7$ the chain-aware task-IL gains are only 0.31 points clean and 0.01 corrupted.
\input{tables/objective}

\paragraph{Learned and random mixture strength.}
Tables~\ref{tab:main} and~\ref{tab:reference} show how the fixed settings compare with learned $m$ and random AugMix. Within MetaMix with classifier InfoNCE, corrupted class-IL is 35.26\% with learned $m$, 42.51\% at fixed $m=0.3$, and 34.60\% at fixed $m=0.9$. Thus the value chosen matters even when $m$ is fixed. AugMix with its original JS coefficient reaches the highest corrupted task-IL mean (84.15\%). Reducing that coefficient to one raises clean class-IL from 45.94\% to 48.99\%, while corrupted class-IL changes from 40.00\% to 39.68\%. At the matched coefficient AugMix exceeds both controllers' best observed JS means on corrupted class-IL.
\input{tables/references}

\paragraph{Which corruptions account for the differences?}
Tables~\ref{tab:class-detail} and~\ref{tab:task-detail} give the results for each corruption. With InfoNCE and $m=0.5$, chain-aware mixing exceeds MetaMix on 13 of 15 class-IL corruption means. The largest advantage is on pixelation (39.74\% versus 39.10\%). MetaMix leads on impulse noise and contrast. Changing the classifier loss within the chain-aware mixer produces larger differences: switching from JS to InfoNCE raises impulse-noise class-IL by 6.63 points but lowers fog class-IL by 3.57 points. When the true task is supplied, the same switch raises accuracy on 14 corruptions, with motion blur the exception (82.33\% with JS and 81.72\% with InfoNCE).

\paragraph{How concentrated are the learned weights?}
At $m=0.1$, the augmentation chains contribute 90\% to the mixed images $\tilde{x}$. For InfoNCE, the most heavily weighted chain receives 38.24\% of that share on average with chain-aware mixing and 34.37\% with MetaMix (Table~\ref{tab:weights}).
\input{tables/diagnostics}

\section{Limitations and Conclusion}
The most consistent gain on corrupted class-IL is from classifier InfoNCE at stronger augmentation. Chain-aware InfoNCE attains the highest observed mean on this metric, while the advantage over MetaMix changes sign across matched strengths. The maximum is selected from five settings on the reported evaluation. With three seeds, dispersion is substantial: at $m=0.1$ the two InfoNCE corrupted class-IL means are $43.89\pm5.68$ and $41.66\pm7.68$ (sample SD). Isolating the effect of candidate descriptors would require holding outer-view sampling and per-view weight sharing fixed. The per-corruption breakdown below covers the original six configurations; the concentration measurements cover $m=0.1$.

\paragraph{Reproducibility.}
The repository includes the \href{https://github.com/Riezmann75/robust-continual-learning/blob/f56de24/scripts/summarize_fixed_m_augmix.py}{three-seed reporting script}, the one-step trainer, and the PBS jobs for the fixed-strength sweep and AugMix coefficient comparison.

